  "sport": "basketball",
  "title": "Heart rate up – two free throws",
  "description": "Players work in pairs, one pair per basket (or two pairs per basket if you have many players). One player sprints from the baseline to half-court and back, goes straight to the free-throw line and shoots two free throws. The partner rebounds and passes back.\n\nEvery miss gives the shooter one extra baseline–half-court–baseline run before the roles switch. Count every make in the team and set a shared target, for example 30 makes in 6 minutes, or count how many of 20 attempts the team makes.\n\nThe point is to find the same routine before every free throw even with the heart racing: breathe out, the same number of bounces, the same look at the rim. Progressions: a longer run (baseline to baseline) or a third free throw that has to go in.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Same routine every time – breathe out and take your bounces."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Don't rush to the line: one calm second there is worth more than a quick start."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Bend your knees – tired shooters fall short, the power comes from the legs."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Run the sprint for real, or you are not practising what happens in a game."
    }
  ],
  "locale": "en",
